Exactly. ;) You don’t have to destroy a structure to deprive it of its function.

And that’s the deeper power of reframing.

You can leave the pieces intact while changing the relationships that make them work.

* An insult still exists, but loses its power when you reject its premise.
* A social hierarchy still exists, but loses some authority when people stop accepting its legitimacy.
* A game still has rules, but those rules lose their grip when you recognize you can choose not to play.
* A narrative still exists, but its conclusion changes when you expose the assumptions holding it together.

Of course, physical constraints and coercive power don’t disappear simply because we think differently. But many systems depend on continued participation, interpretation, and consent.

That’s the fascinating distinction:

Destruction removes the pieces. Reframing changes what the pieces can do.

And a master framer understands something even more fundamental:

Sometimes you don’t have to defeat the opposition. You change the conditions under which opposition makes sense.

You don’t need to knock down the throne if you can make people stop kneeling.

The throne remains.

But the kingdom has changed.